WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.167
-Now, what's all this business
about Duck, Duck, Goose?

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:04.801
-Yeah. No, no, no. We're not
playing Duck, Duck, Goose.

00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:07.667
-No, I just wondered because
I've done "Cats" in my day.

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:09.234
-Yeah. No.
These are different animals.

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:10.934
Does that feel good
that everywhere you go,

00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:12.968
you get a standing ovation?
-Well, that's...

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:15.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.400
I mean, you...

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.234
-It's so nice to know my aunt
and a few of her friends

00:00:19.234 --> 00:00:20.567
are in.
-No, no.

00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:24.000
Can I ask you --
do I call you "Lord"

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.367
or do I call you "Sir"?

00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:27.567
-Let me tell you something.

00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:30.033
"Lord Lloyd Webber"
is a very difficult thing

00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:32.167
to say after 6:00.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.167
[ Laughter ]

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:37.701
-I agree. After 6:00.
So can I call you...?

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:39.801
-Call me Andrew. It's easier.
-I love that.

00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:41.334
Andrew. Hi. Jimmy.
-Andy.

00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:44.934
-Andy. Jimmy. Pleasure.

00:00:44.934 --> 00:00:46.767
This is a big deal.

00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:50.400
Last night was the last
performance after 35 years

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.667
of "The Phantom of the Opera"
on Broadway.

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:54.467
It was fun.
-That was.

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.467
-Final performance on Broadway.

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:00.067
And there you are there.

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:02.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:02.234 --> 00:01:04.834
-Well --
-How --

00:01:04.834 --> 00:01:08.100
How did it feel to be
in the theater yesterday?

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.367
-Well, it was strange, really,
because, you know,

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:12.367
one of the funny things that's
happened with "Phantom" is,

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:15.934
is that over the last few years,
well, few months, really,

00:01:15.934 --> 00:01:18.400
a young audience found it,
and it's --

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.601
I don't know.
It was sad last night

00:01:20.601 --> 00:01:22.934
because I just felt
it could run on.

00:01:22.934 --> 00:01:24.534
But there you go.

00:01:24.534 --> 00:01:27.367
-I think it will live forever.
personally, I mean --

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:29.801
-Well, you never know.
You never know, do you?

00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:31.968
But the great thing
about musicals is,

00:01:31.968 --> 00:01:34.501
is that when everything
comes together,

00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:36.200
which is often very unusual.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.000
It is actually very unusual.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.868
Like, the production,
the whole thing meshes.

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:43.367
It's just one of those moments.

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:44.868
I mean, it happened
with "Lion King."

00:01:44.868 --> 00:01:46.033
It's happened with "Hamilton."

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:47.501
It's happened with me
with "Phantom."

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:50.133
It doesn't happen very often.
-No, and did you know

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:53.734
when you wrote it,
when you did it and you go,

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:54.934
"Oh, this is going to be a hit,

00:01:54.934 --> 00:01:57.501
this is going to run
for 35 years"?

00:01:57.501 --> 00:01:59.434
-No, not really.
I mean, I have to tell you.

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:01.501
In the dress rehearsal we had,

00:02:01.501 --> 00:02:03.300
Hal Prince,
the great legendary director

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.901
Hal Prince,
had this idea that --

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:08.033
Some of you may know
that in "Phantom"

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:11.434
we go up to the roof,
and he had this idea

00:02:11.434 --> 00:02:15.634
that pigeons would fly
from the roof over the audience.

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:18.467
In the dress rehearsal,
they made --

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:20.801
shall we put it this way --
a mess.

00:02:20.801 --> 00:02:23.167
-[ Laughs ] Yeah. 4-D.

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:25.300
-The pigeons left this.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.033
-Yes, well, apparently
that's good luck.

00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:28.634
Apparently, that's good luck.
Yeah.

00:02:28.634 --> 00:02:30.534
-But "Pigeons: the Musical"
I haven't done.

00:02:30.534 --> 00:02:31.567
-We got to work on that.

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:33.167
I'm going to read you
some stats here.

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:34.767
You probably have heard
all of these,

00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:37.000
but just in case anyone wants
to know a little bit of...

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.767
"The Phantom of the Opera"
holds the record

00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:41.300
as the longest-running show
in Broadway history,

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.701
playing 13,981 performances.

00:02:44.701 --> 00:02:47.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.234
The original cast recording

00:02:50.234 --> 00:02:52.000
is certified quadruple platinum

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.634
and is the best-selling
cast recording of all time.

00:02:55.634 --> 00:02:58.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:58.534 --> 00:03:03.701
The orchestra at "Phantom"
was one of Broadway's largest,

00:03:03.701 --> 00:03:05.634
holding 27 full-time musicians,

00:03:05.634 --> 00:03:09.567
11 of whom were with the show
since it first opened.

00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:12.167
And then I like this one a lot.

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:14.767
"The Phantom of the Opera"
has been the largest generator

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:18.501
of income and jobs in Broadway
and U.S. theatrical history.

00:03:18.501 --> 00:03:20.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:24.300
-And let's just say how great
it is to have a live band

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.300
and how great these guys are.
-Yeah, the Roots.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.834
-Just heard them on the way.
-Yeah. Nothing like it.

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:30.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:31.801
I wanted to ask you
about something

00:03:31.801 --> 00:03:33.200
because something
got dropped off

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.267
in my dressing room
earlier today,

00:03:35.267 --> 00:03:36.801
and I wanted
to ask you about it.

00:03:36.801 --> 00:03:39.033
It said,
"Lord Andrew Lloyd Webber,

00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:42.634
care of Mr. James Fallon,
'The Tonight Show'."

00:03:42.634 --> 00:03:45.000
And that made me think
it's from someone.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.167
I haven't opened it.

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:48.367
-Could you open it?

00:03:48.367 --> 00:03:50.734
-Yeah. Of course.
-I can't...

00:03:50.734 --> 00:03:53.067
It's not from the Phantom.

00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:55.834
-Looks like his stationery.

00:03:55.834 --> 00:03:57.167
-Oh, my God.

00:03:57.167 --> 00:04:00.234
-Would you mind...?

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:02.534
-My hands are shaking.

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:04.400
[ Mellow piano music plays ]
I can't believe it.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.667
"Fondest greetings, my Lord.

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:10.267
A title that you will recall
I have obtained for you."

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:11.634
-I like that music.

00:04:11.634 --> 00:04:13.334
-"And to Monsieur James Fallon,

00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:15.467
who I honor
by attaching to the list

00:04:15.467 --> 00:04:18.801
who will receive
my instructions.

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:22.067
I'm extremely displeased
that my legend is no longer

00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:24.367
being told in New York,
a city for which

00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:27.701
I have developed
considerable affection.

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:30.667
I am therefore currently
obtaining a new address

00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:36.167
in which my legend will be
examined in extreme detail.

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:39.501
I advise you and New Yorkers
to be vigilant.

00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:41.634
I remain your obedient servant.
OG."

00:04:41.634 --> 00:04:44.901
Oh, no. I can't bear it.

00:04:44.901 --> 00:04:47.601
-"And New Yorkers
to be vigilant."

00:04:47.601 --> 00:04:49.601
[ Audience oohs ]

00:04:49.601 --> 00:04:52.267
-I can say no more.
I mean -- I mean, all I can say

00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:54.501
is there are no chandeliers
in this building, are there?

00:04:54.501 --> 00:04:57.167
-Just --
No, those are all pigeons.

00:04:57.167 --> 00:05:00.334
-Oh, thank God for that.
Yes, I can see one.

00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:02.434
-Yeah, that's one there.

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:06.234
-Can I ask you about "Cats"?

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:07.934
How does --

00:05:07.934 --> 00:05:12.367
How does one come up
with an idea like "Cats"?

00:05:12.367 --> 00:05:13.601
And are you a cat person?

00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:15.968
-Well, I am a cat person.
Well, I was...

00:05:15.968 --> 00:05:17.400
[ Audience cheers ]

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.334
...a total cat person
until I saw the "Cats" movie.

00:05:20.334 --> 00:05:22.434
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:25.601
-No, you can't --
No! No, you can't...

00:05:25.601 --> 00:05:28.934
-No standing. No standing.

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:30.634
-But what happened
to the "Cats" movie

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:33.167
was during the course
of it being, as it were, shot,

00:05:33.167 --> 00:05:35.801
which I hope the whole movie
would have been --

00:05:35.801 --> 00:05:37.167
But --

00:05:37.167 --> 00:05:38.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.601 --> 00:05:42.701
I bought this little puppy,
and he was called Mojito.

00:05:42.701 --> 00:05:46.167
He's a little Havanese dog.
He comes from Cuba.

00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:47.567
And that's little "Jiti."

00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:49.000
-That's you and Mojito.
-Mojito.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.567
[ Audience awws ]

00:05:51.567 --> 00:05:53.567
And so one of those things --
You know, you're allowed

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:57.634
to take a therapy -- or a dog
like that kind on airplanes.

00:05:57.634 --> 00:05:59.267
You know, you can.
-Oh, sure.

00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:01.734
-But you have to have
a doctor's certificate to do it.

00:06:01.734 --> 00:06:03.367
-Yes.
-You know that.

00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:05.367
So, I forget
which airline it was now,

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:08.167
but I said to the airline,

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:11.534
"I have to have my little
therapy companion dog with me."

00:06:11.534 --> 00:06:13.701
And they said,
"You need a doctor's letter."

00:06:13.701 --> 00:06:15.734
And so I just said,

00:06:15.734 --> 00:06:17.834
"I saw the 'Cats' movie
and bought a puppy."

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:21.868
And they said -- And they said,
"No doctor's letter required."

00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:24.968
[ Laughter ]

00:06:24.968 --> 00:06:26.834
-Mojito can travel.

00:06:26.834 --> 00:06:30.200
-Yes.
-Mojito can travel with you.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:31.868
-Can we talk about
"Bad Cinderella"?

00:06:31.868 --> 00:06:34.133
This is your new project
right here.

00:06:34.133 --> 00:06:37.067
How did you decide to take on
"Cinderella" for your next show?

00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:39.000
Well, it's a very long,
very long story,

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.534
but it's written
by Emerald Fennell,

00:06:40.534 --> 00:06:42.601
who wrote
"Promising Young Woman."

00:06:42.601 --> 00:06:45.701
I've known her for a while.
I mean, she's a -- you know.

00:06:45.701 --> 00:06:47.734
She was a baby
when I first knew her.

00:06:47.734 --> 00:06:49.133
But she had this idea to do

00:06:49.133 --> 00:06:50.968
a kind of
alternative "Cinderella."

00:06:50.968 --> 00:06:52.701
And I liked the idea,
and I just thought

00:06:52.701 --> 00:06:55.701
it'd be fun to do because
it's a fun story, you know?

00:06:55.701 --> 00:06:57.868
And it's one of those things

00:06:57.868 --> 00:07:01.501
where, I don't know,
I just let my hair down a bit.

00:07:01.501 --> 00:07:04.667
And I'm quite -- I haven't
got very much of it left.

00:07:04.667 --> 00:07:07.734
But what I had, I let down.
-Yeah.

00:07:07.734 --> 00:07:10.033
And you hear these songs like --

00:07:10.033 --> 00:07:15.067
If I bring up a show,
can you talk about a song or --

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:16.501
-Well it's quite a good idea.

00:07:16.501 --> 00:07:21.133
I was once in The Peninsula
in Los Angeles

00:07:21.133 --> 00:07:24.033
and very late at night
because I was on English time.

00:07:24.033 --> 00:07:25.634
I went down to the bar.

00:07:25.634 --> 00:07:28.734
And in the bar
was Frank Sinatra.

00:07:28.734 --> 00:07:31.501
And I went over to him, and
we said hello and everything.

00:07:31.501 --> 00:07:34.734
And he said the one great line
of my career.

00:07:34.734 --> 00:07:37.501
The grand piano
was at the other end of the bar.

00:07:37.501 --> 00:07:40.934
And he said to the barman,
"Bring me the grand piano."

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:42.767
[ Laughter ]

00:07:42.767 --> 00:07:46.334
So if you're able to do that...

00:07:46.334 --> 00:07:49.834
-For --
For Lord Andrew Lloyd Webber,

00:07:49.834 --> 00:07:52.133
we can bring you the piano.

00:07:52.133 --> 00:07:54.267
Anything for you.

00:07:54.267 --> 00:07:56.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:56.634 --> 00:07:59.234
Thank you, bud.

00:07:59.234 --> 00:08:01.000
This is...

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.367
♪♪

00:08:05.367 --> 00:08:08.667
We're in for a treat tonight.
This is fantastic.

00:08:08.667 --> 00:08:11.734
Thank you for doing this.
I appreciate this.

00:08:11.734 --> 00:08:13.868
How about
"Phantom of the Opera"?

00:08:13.868 --> 00:08:15.434
-Well, we could do the obvious.

00:08:15.434 --> 00:08:18.467
[ "The Phantom of the Opera"
intro plays ]

00:08:18.467 --> 00:08:19.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:21.734
-Wow. That's it! That's --

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.901
Right now we got
our money's worth.

00:08:23.901 --> 00:08:26.000
I'm very ha-- Already.
-Right. I'm off.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.501
-That's it. Right -- Yeah.

00:08:27.501 --> 00:08:30.534
But you hear that, and you go,
"Oh, I know this is going to be

00:08:30.534 --> 00:08:32.501
something that will stick
in people's heads"?

00:08:32.501 --> 00:08:33.934
-I just thought
it was a great,

00:08:33.934 --> 00:08:35.434
great start
for the whole thing,

00:08:35.434 --> 00:08:38.367
to see the chandelier rise up,
and then you hear those notes.

00:08:38.367 --> 00:08:41.601
Yeah, yeah, yeah. I just --
And it's sometimes music

00:08:41.601 --> 00:08:44.601
and what you think of visually
comes together.

00:08:44.601 --> 00:08:46.133
-How about
"Jesus Christ Superstar"?

00:08:46.133 --> 00:08:48.234
-Well, that was different,
but...

00:08:48.234 --> 00:08:50.167
[ "Superstar" plays ]

00:08:50.167 --> 00:08:52.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:57.467
♪♪

00:08:57.467 --> 00:09:01.767
-That -- That gets -- It gets
a reaction from everybody.

00:09:01.767 --> 00:09:04.267
When you were writing that
or when you wrote that,

00:09:04.267 --> 00:09:07.534
did you go like, "Yeah, this is
'Jesus Christ Superstar'"?

00:09:07.534 --> 00:09:09.000
You go, "That's the lyrics"?

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.501
-Actually, I was going past
a restaurant in London,

00:09:11.501 --> 00:09:13.534
and I was a kid then,

00:09:13.534 --> 00:09:15.367
and I thought --
I got this tune in my head,

00:09:15.367 --> 00:09:17.334
and I went into the restaurant
and I said,

00:09:17.334 --> 00:09:19.234
"Can you please
give me a paper napkin

00:09:19.234 --> 00:09:21.434
and something I could
write this down on?"

00:09:21.434 --> 00:09:23.133
And they said, "Fine."

00:09:23.133 --> 00:09:27.133
And a couple of years later,
I used that.

00:09:27.133 --> 00:09:28.934
-When you have that,
do people go,

00:09:28.934 --> 00:09:31.467
"Oh, we have to make this"?

00:09:31.467 --> 00:09:34.901
How do you sell a musical
about, you know, Jesus?

00:09:34.901 --> 00:09:36.834
-Well, that was a --
[ Laughter ]

00:09:36.834 --> 00:09:39.634
That was a very long story,
and nobody wanted to produce it.

00:09:39.634 --> 00:09:42.234
So it became an album first

00:09:42.234 --> 00:09:45.734
because I was able
to get a record company.

00:09:45.734 --> 00:09:47.901
Tim Rice and I were able
to get a record company

00:09:47.901 --> 00:09:51.267
to basically make
the whole idea.

00:09:51.267 --> 00:09:54.100
So it was produced
a bit like a radio play,

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:57.067
if you could think of that,
but a musical radio play,

00:09:57.067 --> 00:09:59.534
because there was
no visual to it.

00:09:59.534 --> 00:10:01.167
And that's how it started.

00:10:01.167 --> 00:10:03.167
-And then -- But then "Evita."

00:10:03.167 --> 00:10:05.234
-"Evita."
Well, we can think of...

00:10:05.234 --> 00:10:08.367
[ "Don't Cry For Me Argentina"
plays ]

00:10:08.367 --> 00:10:12.801
♪♪

00:10:12.801 --> 00:10:16.634
-Come on! Like, I mean, you
write one of these, I'm happy.

00:10:16.634 --> 00:10:18.634
You write all of these,
you go, "Who are you?!"

00:10:18.634 --> 00:10:20.234
--Well, that's the wonderful
thing about this,

00:10:20.234 --> 00:10:23.033
is it's in D-flat major.
-Of course.

00:10:23.033 --> 00:10:25.200
[ "Don't Cry For Me Argentina"
plays ]

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:26.667
But anyway...

00:10:26.667 --> 00:10:28.033
-But what does that --
What does that mean?

00:10:28.033 --> 00:10:29.534
-Doesn't sound the same in this.

00:10:29.534 --> 00:10:31.534
[ "Don't Cry For Me Argentina"
plays in different key ]

00:10:31.534 --> 00:10:33.667
-No.
-No, it doesn't.

00:10:33.667 --> 00:10:35.133
-Play the other one again.

00:10:35.133 --> 00:10:36.501
[ "Don't Cry For Me Argentina"
plays ]

00:10:36.501 --> 00:10:38.868
-That's it.
That's where it is. Yes.

00:10:38.868 --> 00:10:40.601
-It's all to do with that.
-Wow!

00:10:40.601 --> 00:10:44.033
-And then -- And then "Cats."

00:10:44.033 --> 00:10:45.934
-Well, memory, of course.

00:10:45.934 --> 00:10:47.901
[ "Memory" plays ]

00:10:47.901 --> 00:10:50.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:50.167 --> 00:10:52.701
♪♪

00:10:52.701 --> 00:10:53.834
[ Cymbals crash ]

00:10:53.834 --> 00:10:56.200
-I mean, come on!

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:58.601
-Brilliantly played.
-Yeah.

00:10:58.601 --> 00:11:02.267
Can you give us a taste of
a song from "Bad Cinderella"?

00:11:02.267 --> 00:11:05.067
-Yeah, of course I can.
Let me think. Yes.

00:11:05.067 --> 00:11:07.501
What about this one?
It's called "Lonely You."

00:11:07.501 --> 00:11:14.901
♪♪

00:11:14.901 --> 00:11:22.334
♪♪

00:11:22.334 --> 00:11:29.734
♪♪

00:11:29.734 --> 00:11:32.701
That was wonderful.
If you then take it on...

00:11:32.701 --> 00:11:41.200
♪♪

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:44.100
And it's all about construction
of a melody.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:48.801
♪♪

00:11:48.801 --> 00:11:52.067
And then you hit that note and
then you can do a key change.

00:11:52.067 --> 00:11:55.834
♪♪

00:11:55.834 --> 00:11:58.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:58.067 --> 00:11:59.701
-Yes!

00:11:59.701 --> 00:12:03.834
[ Humming ]

00:12:03.834 --> 00:12:06.367
-And then you can take
the artist up to the top note,

00:12:06.367 --> 00:12:07.734
you see.

00:12:07.734 --> 00:12:12.234
♪♪

00:12:12.234 --> 00:12:14.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:14.267 --> 00:12:16.300
-How -- I don't know.

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:19.534
I'm just honored to meet you
and honored to sit next to you.

00:12:19.534 --> 00:12:21.834
-Well, I'm delighted.

00:12:21.834 --> 00:12:23.968
-Please come back whenever
you're around.

00:12:23.968 --> 00:12:26.367
Thank you very much, indeed.
Thank you.

00:12:26.367 --> 00:12:27.634
-Lord Andrew Lloyd Webber.

00:12:27.634 --> 00:12:29.534
"Bad Cinderella"
is now playing

00:12:29.534 --> 00:12:31.501
at the Imperial Theatre
on Broadway.

00:12:31.501 --> 00:12:34.300
More "Tonight Show" after
the break. Stick around.

